<h2>Is it a real SS 396?</h2>
<p>For 1968, a 138 series points to an SS 396 or Concours V8, which is a good start but not proof. In 1969 the SS 396 became an option package on 300 Deluxe and Malibu models, so the VIN can’t identify an SS at all. Confirm with the engine’s stamped suffix code and partial VIN, the trim tag and paperwork. Note too that the Fisher Body style number on the trim tag (for example 13637) is not the VIN and doesn’t indicate engine type.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does 138 in a Chevelle VIN mean SS 396?</h3><p>In 1968 series 138 covers the SS 396 and the Concours V8. In 1969 the SS 396 was an option package, so the VIN can’t identify it.</p><h3>What does the B in a Chevelle VIN mean?</h3><p>The seventh character is the assembly plant. B is Baltimore, Maryland.</p><h3>What number did 1969 Chevelle VINs start at?</h3><p>1969 Chevelle sequence numbers started at 300001 at the U.S. plants; early Oshawa-built cars began at 100001.</p>
